<h1>Example 4 — <span class="mot-latin">Victimæ paschali laudes</span></h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    The great Easter sequence. With it we change period and style&nbsp;: a later chant,
    closer to the text, and even built like a little dialogue.
</p>

<div class="fiche">
    <dl>
        <dt>Genre</dt><dd>Sequence (<span class="mot-latin">sequentia</span>), sung before the Gospel</dd>
        <dt>Occasion</dt><dd>Easter Day (and its octave)</dd>
        <dt>Author</dt><dd>Attributed to Wipo of Burgundy (11th century)</dd>
        <dt>Mode</dt><dd>Mode I (<em>re</em> mode, at once grave and luminous)</dd>
        <dt>Style</dt><dd>Rather <strong>syllabic</strong>&nbsp;: often one note per syllable</dd>
        <dt>In the <em>Triplex</em></dt><dd>p. 198</dd>
    </dl>
</div>

<div class="lecteur-audio">
    <span class="etiquette">Listen to the chant</span>
    <p class="note-audio">Follow the dialogue «&nbsp;Dic nobis Maria…&nbsp;» by ear&nbsp;: question, then answer.</p>
