<h2>The text and its translation</h2>
<div class="texte-latin">
    <div class="entete lat">Latin</div>
    <div class="entete fr">English</div>
    <div class="lat">Victimæ paschali laudes<br>immolent Christiani.</div>
    <div class="fr">To the paschal victim<br>let Christians offer their praises.</div>
    <div class="lat">Agnus redemit oves&nbsp;:<br>Christus innocens Patri reconciliavit peccatores.</div>
    <div class="fr">The Lamb has redeemed the sheep&nbsp;;<br>Christ the innocent has reconciled sinners with the Father.</div>
    <div class="lat">Mors et vita duello<br>conflixere mirando&nbsp;: dux vitæ mortuus, regnat vivus.</div>
    <div class="fr">Death and life fought<br>a prodigious duel&nbsp;: the Lord of life, having died, reigns alive.</div>
    <div class="lat"><span class="chip">Question</span> Dic nobis Maria,<br>quid vidisti in via&nbsp;?</div>
    <div class="fr">Tell us, Mary,<br>what did you see on the way&nbsp;?</div>
    <div class="lat"><span class="chip">Answer</span> Sepulcrum Christi viventis,<br>et gloriam vidi resurgentis…</div>
    <div class="fr">The tomb of the living Christ,<br>and I saw the glory of the Risen One…</div>
    <div class="lat">Scimus Christum surrexisse a mortuis vere&nbsp;:<br>tu nobis, victor Rex, miserere. Amen. Alleluia.</div>
    <div class="fr">We know that Christ is truly risen from the dead&nbsp;;<br>have mercy on us, victorious King. Amen. Alleluia.</div>
</div>

<h2>What is a sequence&nbsp;?</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    Our first three examples go back to the heart of the old Gregorian repertoire. The
    <strong>sequence</strong>, by contrast, is a later genre (from the 9th–11th century) and
    of a different spirit. It is a <strong>strophic</strong> chant&nbsp;: it proceeds in
    pairs of phrases (<em>verses</em>) which, two by two, share the same melody. One idea is
    sung, then taken up again to the same tune with a new text.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — syllabic</span>
    <p>
        A <strong>syllabic</strong> chant sets (most often) <em>a single note per
        syllable</em>. It is the opposite of the teeming melisma of <a href="{{
        path('paleo_en_viderunt') }}">Viderunt omnes</a>. As a result, the text remains very
        intelligible — one «&nbsp;follows&nbsp;» the words easily.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>What changes when reading the neumes</h2>
<p>
    Good news&nbsp;: because the sequence is largely syllabic, the neumes are easier to spot
    here&nbsp;! You will mostly meet isolated <strong>puncta</strong> (one note per
    syllable), punctuated now and then by a <strong>pes</strong> or a
    <strong>clivis</strong> at important joints. It is an excellent chant for
    <em>consolidating</em> what you have learned, without the density of the graduals.
</p>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">Listen for&nbsp;: the dialogue</span>
    <p>
        Find the passage <span class="mot-latin">«&nbsp;Dic nobis Maria…&nbsp;»</span>
        («&nbsp;Tell us, Mary…&nbsp;»). The text stages a <strong>question</strong> put to
        Mary Magdalene and her <strong>answer</strong>. The melody underlines this
        dialogue&nbsp;: it is a little sung theatre, almost a scene. Notice how, in this
        passage, the neumes stay sober so as to let the words speak.
    </p>
</div>

